        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-2657bf026adaa944\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Prince Vassily’s embedded «я люблю вас» presents his affection for Anna Mikhailovna as true, specifically as a state to be demonstrated by granting her request. The love clause is therefore strongly truth-conditional even if socially strategic or insincere. It weakly invokes their relational history and obligation, but the explicit undertaking lies chiefly in the accompanying promise to secure her son’s transfer, not in the love avowal itself. Nothing indicates reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal may be conventional, exaggerated, or strategically deployed rather than sincere; this affects its contextual interpretation, not its truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Prince Vassily directly addresses Anna Mikhailovna, with the love expression grammatically embedded in «чтобы доказать вам, как я люблю вас» (‘to prove to you how I love you’).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal helps frame a reluctant concession as evidence of affection and respect for Anna Mikhailovna’s late father. Narration emphasizes Vassily’s boredom and practical calculation: conscience and his wish to avoid continued pestering sway him. Thus the phrase is socially strategic and possibly conventional, while still presenting love as a fact. His concrete promise supplies the scene’s principal commitment force but should not be conflated with the core love clause.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"«чтобы доказать вам, как я люблю вас и чту память покойного отца вашего» (‘to prove to you how I love you and honor the memory of your late father’)\",\"supports\":\"The embedded clause explicitly presents Vassily’s love as an existing state whose reality his action will demonstrate, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He speaks «с своею всегдашнею фамильярностью и скукой в голосе» (‘with his customary familiarity and boredom in his voice’).\",\"supports\":\"The bored, familiar delivery weighs against interpreting the words as expressive or reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator says Vassily is swayed partly by a pang of conscience and partly by realizing that Anna Mikhailovna will otherwise continue pestering him and make scenes.\",\"supports\":\"His concession and affectionate framing are calculated responses to social pressure; this contextual strategy does not remove the utterance’s T force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"«я сделаю невозможное: сын ваш будет переведен в гвардию, вот вам моя рука» (‘I shall do the impossible: your son will be transferred to the Guards; here is my hand on it’).\",\"supports\":\"A genuine undertaking accompanies the love avowal, creating slight P pressure through the proof-of-love construction, although the promise rather than «я люблю вас» carries most of that force.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately distinguishes the asserted affection from the adjacent promise and from the scene’s strategic, emotionally undemonstrative delivery. No residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
